  "id": "Compound::DB00983 - Gene::153",
  "verbalization": "Formoterol, Pirbuterol, and Salbutamol are all classified as adrenergic beta-2 agonists, and each of these compounds binds to the Adrb1 receptor. This binding is a key mechanism by which these drugs exert their physiological effects. The shared classification as adrenergic beta-2 agonists suggests a common pathway of action, where the drugs interact with the same receptor subtype, Adrb1, to modulate cellular signaling. The evidence from the three paths converges on the notion that Formoterol, through its inclusion in the adrenergic beta-2 agonist class, ultimately binds to Adrb1, thereby supporting a direct relationship between these compounds and the receptor. The low common ancestor of Formoterol, Pirbuterol, and Salbutamol as Beta-Adrenergic Agonists further reinforces this shared mechanism, highlighting the structural and functional similarities that underpin their pharmacological actions.",
